{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== ADDENDUM: THE HISTORY SIGNAL ALREADY EXISTS, IS ALREADY PERSISTED, AND IS ALREADY GOVERNED BY AN ADR ===\n\nCorrecting my own cost estimate in the previous comment (I proposed widening `.bridge_state/prev.json`).\nThere is a better home, and it makes the \"build it correctly\" option materially cheaper than either that\nproposal or the plan's rejected alternative. Traced in the code, not inferred:\n\n**ADR 0026's per-binding BASELINE — \"the last-synced Jira-side values\" — stored on the binding entry.**\n  * `binding_store.set_baseline(local_id, fields)` (`binding_store.py:453-467`) records it; the entry key\n    is `baseline`; it is **in-memory until `save()`, persisted by the EXISTING binding-store commit path,\n    \"no new commit surface (ADR 0026 §Consequences)\"**.\n  * `reconcile._advance_baselines` (`reconcile.py:519-536`) runs **EVERY PASS**, advancing every\n    `confirmed` binding whose key is in the current fetch window to the current snapshot. So the\n    artifact is not just present — it is already maintained on exactly the cadence this arbitration\n    needs.\n  * **The absence semantics are ALREADY the fail-safe we need, and already documented:** \"An ABSENT\n    baseline is VALID and degrades to local-wins (safe/lossy); a version-1 store (no baselines) reads\n    fine\" (`binding_store.py:101-105`). No baseline -> no proof the peer ever had the link -> no removal.\n    That is the migration story, already written, already shipped.\n\nSo the arbitration becomes a HISTORY comparison with no new artifact and no new commit surface:\n  * link in the binding's last-synced peer link set, and ABSENT from the current OBSERVED set\n      -> the peer DELETED it -> inbound REMOVE wins, and the outbound ADD for that pair is suppressed.\n  * link NOT in the baseline -> we have never seen it on the peer -> **it is a local link we have not\n      pushed** -> outbound ADD wins, no removal. Brand-new deps are safe BY CONSTRUCTION, which is what\n      the amended plan claimed and could not deliver.\n  * baseline absent (v1 store, unconfirmed binding, out-of-window key) -> local-wins -> no removal.\n  * current enrichment unobserved -> G1 already declines, unchanged.\n\n`managed_refs` then drops OUT of the arbitration entirely — which is the right outcome, because it\ncannot answer the question being asked. Keep G3 if desired as a belt-and-braces gate; do not let it\ndecide.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION NOTES for whoever writes the amendment:\n  * store the peer link set under its OWN key on the binding entry with its own accessor — do NOT add\n    `issuelinks` to `_BASELINE_FIELDS` (`binding_store.py:106-112`), which is the 5-scalar three-way-merge\n    vocabulary consumed by `outbound_field_diff` and `binding_walk`; widening it would perturb that merge.\n  * store it in LOCAL RELATION vocabulary (`[[relation, local_target_id], ...]`) via the same\n    `link_direction` translation both differs already use, so the comparison never touches raw peer keys\n    (the direction/vocabulary trap the plan already documents).\n  * only advance the recorded set when the current pass OBSERVED issuelinks for that key (G1 semantics),\n    or a failed enrichment would overwrite a good history with an empty one and manufacture the very\n    \"peer deleted everything\" reading G1 exists to prevent. **This is the load-bearing detail of the\n    whole design — a fail-open READ must not be allowed to poison the history.**\n  * plumbing: `prev_snapshot` reaches `outbound_field_diff` today but NOT the link differs, whereas\n    `binding_store` is ALREADY passed to both `_diff_links`/`_diff_link_removals` and\n    `_diff_link_removals_inbound`. That asymmetry is a second reason to prefer the baseline over the\n    snapshot: **the arbitration needs no new parameter on either differ.**\n\nCOST, honestly: one new binding-entry field + accessor, one advance site next to `_advance_baselines`\n(gated on observation), the arbitration in both differs, plus the ledger and a harness run. Bigger than\na one-line guard flip; much smaller than the \"new persisted artifact with its own migration and\ntruncation semantics\" the plan declined on.\n\nThis does not decide the ticket — deferring the feature and documenting local-wins is still a legitimate\ncall, since today's landed behaviour fails safe. It only corrects the price of building it right."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785771442792648001,"uuid":"1d3a3fdf-d6e2-4fcb-9bd1-28577869a3d1"}